        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\n1. This is already possible via creating buffer-local mappings in dedicated event, as described in #879, #534, and #409. The solution is similar in spirit as the [one described in examples](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/919b953f044d97a2243cc2d1f0b316a199434127/doc/mini-files.txt#L445) for a different purpose.\r\n2. Yes, it is possible by creating buffer-local mappings together with \"disabling\" creating a default mapping via `config.mappings.go_in = ''`. The tabpage part of the question is possible via changing the target window (i.e. the window inside which the buffer for the file will be shown). See [this example](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/919b953f044d97a2243cc2d1f0b316a199434127/doc/mini-files.txt#L473). You can change the logic of target window change to your liking. Also, couple of clarifications here:\r\n    - The default behavior of `go_in()` in case a file is already opened in *some* buffer is to show that *same* buffer in the target window. Not \"open file in a new buffer\".\r\n    - My suggestion would be to override the `go_in_plus` behavior leaving `go_in` untouched for common usage (but that is a matter of taste).\r\n\r\nClosing as both duplicate and already possible.",
            "created_at": "2024-10-27T11:40:45Z",
